<Project>
  <!--
    Flows into apps that consume the CupriFace.Android NUPKG (repo-internal apps set this in
    their own csproj — buildTransitive does not cross ProjectReference edges).

    CoreCLR is the supported runtime, and this default is load-bearing, not advisory: the Mono
    runtime pack shipped with .NET 10 (10.0.11) has a code-generation defect that crashes
    CupriFace apps during startup — a native SIGSEGV inside Mono-compiled CoreLib where string
    CONTENT is dereferenced as a string REFERENCE. It reproduces on arm64 hardware and x64
    emulators alike, with AOT and JIT alike (they share Mono's code generator), trimmed or not.
    Full forensics and the upstream-ready report: samples/AndroidProbe/MONO-CRASH.md in the
    CupriFace repository. Re-evaluate when the Mono runtime pack moves past 10.0.11 — the
    AndroidProbe workflow's variant matrix exists to answer exactly that.

    THIS FILE IS .props, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT (#172). The same PropertyGroup lived in the
    package's .targets for its entire life and never once fired, because of when the two are
    imported:

      1. Sdk.props, package buildTransitive/*.props, Directory.Build.props   ← .props lands HERE
      2. the project body
      3. Sdk.targets → the Android workload's DefaultProperties.targets:
             <UseMonoRuntime Condition=" '$(UseMonoRuntime)' == '' ">true</UseMonoRuntime>
      4. NuGet's generated .nuget.g.targets → package buildTransitive/*.targets  ← .targets landed HERE

    By step 4 the property is already 'true', so a guard of "'$(UseMonoRuntime)' == ''" can never
    be true and the pin was a no-op. Right shape, wrong file. From step 1 the guard holds, and the
    Android SDK's own "== ''" condition at step 3 then correctly declines to overwrite it.

    Nothing in this repository caught it: every Android app here (AndroidViewer, AndroidLottie,
    AndroidProbe, GlProbe.Android) sets UseMonoRuntime in its own csproj, so none of them has ever
    exercised the buildTransitive path. The guard against that is a package-consumer build in CI,
    which is what tests/PackageConsumer exists for.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- The marker is set FIRST, while UseMonoRuntime is still empty, because it records whether
         THIS PACKAGE is the reason the app is on CoreCLR. An app that chose CoreCLR itself gets no
         explanation from us; it already has one. The .targets beside this file uses it to explain
         the XA1040 warning the choice produces. -->
    <CupriFacePinnedCoreClr Condition="'$(UseMonoRuntime)' == ''">true</CupriFacePinnedCoreClr>
    <UseMonoRuntime Condition="'$(UseMonoRuntime)' == ''">false</UseMonoRuntime>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
